## What I did (change #3)

Applied the same trace-safety refactoring to `BaseStrokeContent.draw()` in `lottie/src/main/java/com/airbnb/lottie/animation/content/BaseStrokeContent.java`:
- Wrapped the body in `try { ... } finally { L.endSection("StrokeContent#draw") }`.
- Three duplicated `endSection` sites (two early returns + the end) collapse to one. Braces balance (88/88).

## But the important part is what I *didn't* change, and why

You asked me to apply this to "the `draw()` methods," so I checked all of them — and only one was actually a safe fit. I want to be explicit about the rest rather than quietly transforming them:

- **`BaseLayer.draw()` — deliberately skipped (would be a bug).** Its outer trace section closes *non-uniformly*: the normal exits call `recordRenderTime(L.endSection(...))`, but the hidden-layer early return calls bare `L.endSection(...)`. A uniform `finally` would have started feeding render-time samples for skipped hidden layers into the performance tracker — a real behavior change disguised as a cleanup. This is exactly the entanglement I flagged last turn, and it turned out to be present.
- **`FillContent.draw()`, `GradientFillContent.draw()`, `CompositionLayer.drawLayer()` — nothing to do.** Their early returns sit *before* `beginSection`, so the traced region already has a single exit. Wrapping them would add noise without removing any duplication.

So the honest tally for this request is **one** draw method refactored, three correctly left alone. Mechanically applying the transform to all of them would have introduced a regression in `BaseLayer`.

## Status / caveats (unchanged)

- Still **no Android SDK here**, so none of the three changes have been compiled or run. Please run `./gradlew :lottie:testDebugUnitTest` and the **snapshot tests** before merging — the snapshot suite is the real safety net for anything touching `draw()`.
- All three changes are behavior-preserving on normal paths and touch no public API.

If you'd like, I can also apply the identical fix to the non-`draw()` methods in `BaseStrokeContent` that share the pattern (`applyTrimPath`, `applyDashPatternIfNeeded`), or stop here and let you review/run the tests. Which do you prefer?

